+===============================================================================
+SPHINX PYTHON DOMAIN FIX - COMPLETE VERIFICATION REPORT
+===============================================================================
+Date: 2026-09-23
+Repository: /testbed (Sphinx source code)
+
+===============================================================================
+PROBLEM SUMMARY
+===============================================================================
+
+Issue: False "more than one target found" warnings when using :type: or :rtype: 
+fields in Python docstrings.
+
+Symptom: When a class name exists in both a parent module and its submodules 
+(e.g., mod.A and mod.submod.A), Sphinx incorrectly reports ambiguity warnings 
+even when the context clearly indicates which class should be used.
+
+Example:
+  Module structure:
+    - mod.A (class in parent module)
+    - mod.submod.A (class in submodule)
+  
+  Documentation in 'mod':
+    .. py:currentmodule:: mod
+    .. py:function:: func(a)
+       :type a: A        <-- Should resolve to mod.A, but warns about ambiguity
+       :rtype: A         <-- Should resolve to mod.A, but warns about ambiguity
+
+===============================================================================
+ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS
+===============================================================================
+
+File: /testbed/sphinx/domains/python.py
+Function: PythonDomain.find_obj() (around lines 1269-1273)
+
+The fuzzy search mode (searchmode=1) implementation:
+
+```python
+# "fuzzy" searching mode
+searchname = '.' + name
+matches = [(oname, self.objects[oname]) for oname in self.objects
+           if oname.endswith(searchname) and
+           self.objects[oname].objtype in objtypes]
+```
+
+This returns ALL objects whose fully qualified name ends with '.name', without
+considering the module hierarchy context. This causes legitimate references to
+be flagged as ambiguous.
+
+Key Discovery: The difference between :py:class:`A` and :type: A
+- :py:class:`A` uses PyXrefMixin.make_xref() which sets refspecific=True
+- :type: A uses type_to_xref() which does NOT set refspecific=True
+- Both eventually call find_obj() with searchmode=1
+- The issue is in find_obj()'s fuzzy search, not in the calling code
+
+===============================================================================
+SOLUTION IMPLEMENTED
+===============================================================================
+
+Modified: /testbed/sphinx/domains/python.py (lines 1274-1286)
+
+Added module-aware prioritization logic to the fuzzy search:
+
+```python
+# if there are multiple matches, prefer matches in the current module
+if len(matches) > 1 and modname:
+    # first try to find an exact match in the current module
+    exact_match = [(oname, obj) for oname, obj in matches
+                 if oname == modname + searchname]
+    if exact_match:
+        matches = exact_match
+    else:
+        # if no exact match, look for matches in submodules
+        module_matches = [(oname, obj) for oname, obj in matches
+                        if oname.startswith(modname + '.')]
+        if module_matches:
+            matches = module_matches
+```
+
+Strategy:
+1. Prefer exact matches in the current module (modname.name)
+2. If no exact match, use matches in submodules (modname.*)
+3. If no module context match, return all matches (preserves original behavior)
+
